## Actor programs

Inspect types with `typescript_api`; call `context.functions` in `typescript_eval` or an actor
program. Use snippets for one-time work, programs for persistent state, later inputs or views.

Actors can own TypeScript functions, journaled state and React views. An LLM actor still uses
its model for ActorInputs; a TypeScript actor can handle them with `onInput`. Calling a function
from a view executes that function directly and does not start a model turn.

1. Create the closest template with `actor_program_create`; read and reuse its package, contract,
   implementation, view and tests under `@actors/<name>/`. Language servers use that alias;
   Bash uses `$RAGENTS_ACTORS_DIR/<name>/`.
2. Edit with ordinary file tools; preserve imports and structure. Changed errors arrive before
   model requests; `actor_program_diagnostics` returns the full last check.
3. Adapt meaningful `node:test` cases in `tests/**/*.test.ts`. Query `actor_program_controls`
   for unclear props. Templates demonstrate controls; pure views need no server function.
4. `actor_program_activate` typechecks, builds, tests and activates. Fix errors and reactivate.
   List, removal and visibility have separate functions.

Published functions enter the shared API in the same turn. Inspect with `typescript_api`, call
`context.functions.<name>(input)`. Reactivation updates the contract; removal withdraws it.
Native tool exposure is optional and explicit.

Use `actor: "self"` or `actor: "@handle"` to bind to an existing actor. Without a binding, a
backend creates a TypeScript actor with the program name; a pure view uses the calling actor.
Reactivation preserves the existing binding. Do not create a dummy actor for a pure view.
An LLM actor cannot receive an `onInput` backend; its inputs remain model work. Each actor owns
one active program package; add further functions and views to that package.

`package.json` contains `name`, `private: true`, `type: "module"` and `ragents` metadata:
`title`, optional `description`, optional `backend`, and optional `views`. Each view has an
`id`, `client`, optional `title`, `styles`, `width` and `height`. At least a backend or a view
is required. The host supplies the HTML root and fixed local dependencies.

Import `defineActor` from `@ragents/server` and `Type` from `typebox`. Its contract contains
`state`, `functions` and optional `input: { capabilities }`. Each function declares `label`,
`input`, `output` and optional `capabilities`; `tool: { name, targets?, card? }` exposes the same
function in the shared API. Omitted targets mean all active executable actors; `self` means the owner.
Export `defineActor(contract, { functions: { ... }, onInput? })` as default. Callbacks receive
`(input, context)`; returned values are results. Only `context.state.replace` changes actor state.
The state schema must accept the initial `{}`. `context.actor` identifies the owning actor.
Server code calls run functions through `context.functions`; declared `capabilities` select the
functions it needs. An `onInput` acts as its actor. A function owns that actor's state but calls
run functions as its caller. A subscription therefore belongs to the caller; initialize an
actor's own event handling through its `onInput`. Snippets act as their caller.

Import React and `createRoot` normally. Import `context` and `useAppState` from `@ragents/client`
and controls from `@ragents/client/ui`: shadcn/ui components (`Button`, `Select`, `Tabs`, `Dialog`,
`Badge`, `Input`, `Table`, ...) with their usual props, plus `Form`, `DataTable`, `FilePicker`,
`AppLayout`, `Stack`, `Grid` and chat. Icons: `lucide-react`; Tailwind works.
The hook reads actor state; `context.capabilities.call`
calls its declared functions. Local drafts remain React state. A real chat binds to the owning
actor through `context.actor.handle`; sending a chat message deliberately starts its next turn.
Use `AppLayout` for title, description, content and actions, `Stack` for spacing or rows,
and `Grid` for columns responsive to frame width. `AppLayout fill` needs a height-constrained
parent: content scrolls while header and actions remain visible. Mount in the supplied `#root`.
The host supplies fonts, box sizing, page size, scrolling and outer padding. Do not reset
`html`, `body` or `#root`, or add outer padding; style only content.
Use `Form` for labeled inputs and interview steps, `type: "textarea"` for long answers.
It handles layout, validation and async `onSubmit`. Keep progression in app state; avoid fixed
field heights and absolute form layouts. Where Form does not fit, use `Input`, `Textarea`,
`Field` and `Label`. Do not duplicate control colors, borders or fonts.
Share steps, prompts and freedoms via `defineWorkflow` from `@ragents/workflow`.
Compose prompts with `workflowInstructions` and server-only `readPrompt` from
`@ragents/workflow/prompts`. Render `UI.WorkflowDiagram` with definition, state and label;
The actor enforces completion and extensions; see learning-afternoon.
Use `FlowDiagram` for other graphs (star layout, fit, actions). Keep local IDs stable.
See `controls`. For custom SVG use `SvgEdge`, Tailwind utilities, responsive `viewBox` and
accessible descriptions.
Address view visibility with `package-name/view-key`, `@handle/view-key` or a unique title;
canvas nodes and lines use the same names with the `app:` prefix.
The host controls resizing, collapse and full view. Views appear on the canvas; owner cards
start hidden but remain in the Actors list. Personal actor visibility is separate from
`actor_view_set_visibility` and cannot be changed by layouts.

Tests call `program.functions.<name>(input, context)` or `program.onInput(input, context)`.
`createTestContext` from `@ragents/server/testing` supplies state and explicit typed `functions` mocks.
Inspect results and `context.state.read()` separately. Subscription delivery is an ActorInput;
`onInput` uses the same state and function API as the actor's functions.
